1. WHO MAY USE IT

You may install and run this software, as supplied, free of charge, if
you are:

  (a) an individual person, using it for your own purposes; or

  (b) a charity, registered as such in its own country, using it for
      its charitable purposes.

You may make the copies needed to install, run and back up your own
installation. Configuring the software is not modifying it.


2. WHO MAY NOT

Nobody else. Without limiting that, the following have no permission,
whether or not they charge anyone for anything:

  (a) a company, firm, partnership or other business, including where
      it runs this software only on its own network and sells nothing;

  (b) a government or any part of one, at any level;

  (c) any organisation funded by the state, including a school,
      college, university, hospital or other institution so funded.

An organisation that is a charity under 1(b) has permission, whatever
else it is, and whether or not it receives state funding. Charity
registration is the test, not where the money comes from: a registered
charity that is largely funded by government grants is permitted, and so
is an independently funded school that is a registered charity. An
organisation that is not a registered charity is still excluded by 2(c)
if the state funds it.


3. WHAT YOU MAY NOT DO

The permission above is to run the software as supplied. You may not:

  (a) modify, adapt, translate or build anything derived from it;

  (b) distribute, publish, share, lend, sell or otherwise supply it to
